import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Effect resumption semantics — perform/handle as algebra homomorphisms on free arrow-algebras","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Effect resumption semantics — `perform`/`handle` as algebra homomorphisms on free arrow-algebras","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed","status_note":"Proposed — one-shot in-flight dispatch landed; multi-shot `resume` landed via replay journals on all three engines (`design/multishot-resume.md`)","refs":["ADR-0153","ADR-0154"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0158-effect-resumption-semantics.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0158-effect-resumption-semantics.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0158-effect-resumption-semantics.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0158-effect-resumption-semantics-—-perform-handle-as-algebra-homomorphisms-on-free-arrow-algebras" tabindex="-1">ADR-0158: Effect resumption semantics — <code>perform</code>/<code>handle</code> as algebra homomorphisms on free arrow-algebras <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0158-effect-resumption-semantics-—-perform-handle-as-algebra-homomorphisms-on-free-arrow-algebras" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0158: Effect resumption semantics — perform/handle as algebra homomorphisms on free arrow-algebras”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Refs</strong>: ADR-0153 (dimensions/<code>~[n]~&gt;</code>), ADR-0154 (typecheck semantics), Sanada — <em>Algebraic effects and handlers for arrows</em> (ICFP/JFP 2024); <code>design/semantics.md</code> — the language-wide denotational reading this ADR&#39;s correspondence table anchors</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>Effects landed as <em>trace dispatch</em>: <code>perform(op, arg)</code> mints <code>IO&lt;{op: arg}&gt;</code> — a record of ops-performed; <code>handle</code> interprets the produced record entry-by-entry after the fact. That model works but is one semantic step short of the algebraic model: there is no suspension point, so ops cannot feed results back into the arrow, and state-threading effects (State) need chained single-op IO workarounds (dogfood-2 finding).</p><p>Sanada&#39;s <em>algebraic effects and handlers for arrows</em> is the same surface (ops + handlers on the Lindley–Wadler–Yallop arrow calculus) with resumption semantics: an op suspends the arrow, the handler&#39;s clause produces the resumption value, execution continues at the op site.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong><code>perform</code> dispatches in-flight.</strong> <code>perform(op, arg)</code> under an enclosing <code>handle</code> resolves the innermost handler frame covering <code>op</code>, calls <code>h[op](arg)</code>, and its return value <em>is</em> the <code>perform</code> result — the arrow resumes. Uncovered ops mint the residual <code>IO&lt;{op: arg}&gt;</code> trace exactly as today: the trace semantics become the <em>unhandled</em> case, not the only one.</p><p><strong><code>handle f with h</code> means</strong>: while <code>f</code> executes, <code>f</code>&#39;s <code>perform</code>s interpret through <code>h</code>; ops <code>h</code> doesn&#39;t cover remain residual. The type-level sig <code>IO&lt;{puts: T}&gt;</code> is unchanged — it records what the arrow <em>does</em>, independent of who interprets it.</p><p><strong><code>handle f with h acc s0</code></strong> — stateful handlers via the arrow-calculus <code>loop</code>/trace combinator: <code>acc</code> is the <code>c</code> wire of <code>loop ((args, s) ~&gt; (result, s&#39;))</code>. Handler impl signature: <code>(payload, s) ~&gt; (result, s&#39;)</code>. The accumulator is the trace wire, not a special frame slot.</p><p><strong><code>return</code> clause</strong> — handlers gain <code>return: r ~&gt; e</code> — the algebra&#39;s unit map on the arrow&#39;s pure result. Completes the algebra shape.</p><p><strong>Handlers are 2-cells.</strong> A handler maps each op-generator to a real arrow — a structure-preserving interpretation of the free arrow-algebra on the signature, i.e. an algebra homomorphism; in the arrow/cell vocabulary that&#39;s a <code>~[2]~&gt;</code>-shaped transformation between interpretations. Handler <em>laws</em> are cell equations, checkable with <code>~[2]~&gt;</code>/<code>~[3]~&gt;</code> machinery:</p><div class="language-hnl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">hnl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>-- State law: set-then-read returns the set value</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>law_state : (put(v) &gt;&gt;&gt; get()) ~[2]~&gt; pure(v)</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="one-decl-x-gen-result-—-data-removed-io-internal" tabindex="-1">One decl: <code>X := { gen : … ~&gt; result }</code> — <code>data ::</code> removed, <code>IO</code> internal <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-decl-x-gen-result-—-data-removed-io-internal" aria-label="Permalink to “One decl: X := { gen : … ~&gt; result } — data :: removed, IO internal”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Generator tables are records.</strong> A <code>X := { field : type }</code> record where fields&#39; types reference the bound name is a former declaration; the field&#39;s codomain shape decides the reading:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>field codomain</th><th>the field IS</th><th>fold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>X&lt;…&gt;</code> (the former)</td><td>a <code>data</code> ctor</td><td><code>match</code>/<code>X.rec</code></td></tr><tr><td>own <code>B</code> (doesn&#39;t return <code>X</code>)</td><td>an <code>IO</code> op (suspends)</td><td><code>handle</code></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="language-hnl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">hnl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>-- data (was: data Nat :: Zero | Succ(pred : Nat)):</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Nat := { Zero : Nat, Succ : Nat ~&gt; Nat }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- indexed (was: data Vec(n : Nat, a : Type) :: VNil | VCons(...)):</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Vec&lt;n, a&gt; := {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  VNil  : Vec&lt;0, a&gt;,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  VCons : (k : Nat, x : a, xs : Vec&lt;k, a&gt;) ~&gt; Vec&lt;Succ k, a&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- &lt;n,a&gt; on the LHS = family telescope (the existing implicit-binder pos)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- effects — the record IS the sig:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>State := { get : Unit ~&gt; Nat, put : Nat ~&gt; Unit }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>counter : Unit ~&gt; IO&lt;State&gt;        -- or ~&gt; State if the marker drops</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong><code>data X :: C | D</code> is removed</strong> — the record form covers it including dependent ctors (field names live in the arrow domain). The <code>::</code>/<code>|</code> list form was <code>data</code>-flavored sugar that implied ctors-returning-the- former — the record literal <em>is</em> the generator table, so <code>:=</code> is the honest spelling. Migration: <code>.hnl</code> sources rewrite <code>data X :: C(args)</code> → <code>X := { C : (args) ~&gt; X }</code> — mechanical, <code>types.yaml</code>/<code>desugar</code> reads the same telescope.</p><p><strong><code>IO</code> becomes an internal marker</strong> — <code>IO&lt;S&gt;</code> marks &quot;read <code>S</code>&#39;s fields as suspensions&quot; at the type level (vs <code>~&gt; S</code> reading the record). May shrink to implementation detail if <code>handle</code>-position carries the interpretation; the user-facing decl is just <code>S := {…}</code>.</p><p>The <code>data</code>/<code>effect</code> decls are <strong>one mechanism</strong> — &quot;these are the generators&quot;; the only difference is what a generator produces:</p><ul><li>codomain <code>X&lt;…&gt;</code> → a <em>value</em> node (<code>{__ctor__}</code>); <code>match</code>/<code>X.rec</code> folds a finished spine.</li><li>codomain <code>B</code> (not <code>X</code>) → a <em>suspension</em> node (<code>{op, arg, next}</code>); <code>handle</code> folds the spine in-flight.</li></ul><p><code>match</code> on a finished value and <code>handle</code> on a live spine are the same eliminator read at different moments; the full collapse (<code>handle</code> literally <em>is</em> <code>F.rec</code> on the op-spine) completes when <code>next</code>/resumption is a first-class value — the multi-shot <code>resume</code> workstream.</p><h2 id="correspondence-sanada-→-handl" tabindex="-1">Correspondence (Sanada → handl) <a class="header-anchor" href="#correspondence-sanada-→-handl" aria-label="Permalink to “Correspondence (Sanada → handl)”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Sanada</th><th>handl</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Operation <code>op : A ⇝ B</code> (signature arrow)</td><td><code>perform(op, x)</code> — invokes the <code>op</code> generator at <code>x</code></td></tr><tr><td>Signature Σ</td><td><code>IO&lt;{ op: A ~&gt; B, … }&gt;</code> — record-indexed former</td></tr><tr><td>Free arrow-algebra on Σ</td><td>arrow <code>A ~&gt; IO&lt;Σ&gt;</code> whose body performs ops</td></tr><tr><td>Handler = A-algebra homomorphism</td><td><code>handle f with { op: impl, return: r ~&gt; … }</code></td></tr><tr><td>Algebra carrier / fold state</td><td><code>acc s</code> — the <code>loop</code> trace wire</td></tr><tr><td>Clause continuation</td><td>the <code>perform</code> call site — one-shot (direct style: resume = the call returning)</td></tr><tr><td>Denotational: strong promonad A in Prof</td><td>operational: <code>handler_stack</code> frames + <code>interpret_io_former</code></td></tr><tr><td>2-cells between interpretations</td><td><code>~[2]~&gt;</code>/<code>~[3]~&gt;</code> — same machinery</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="deltas-from-sanada-honest" tabindex="-1">Deltas from Sanada (honest) <a class="header-anchor" href="#deltas-from-sanada-honest" aria-label="Permalink to “Deltas from Sanada (honest)”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Multi-shot resumption by replay, not stack capture.</strong> A clause may take a second parameter — the resumption <code>(payload, resume) ~&gt; …</code> — and <code>resume</code> is a first-class, copyable value: nondeterminism (<code>choose</code>), generators (<code>yield</code>). Rather than capturing the continuation (costly on bytecode/cranelift), <code>resume(x)</code> <strong>re-runs the handled body</strong> from the <code>handle</code> boundary under a replay journal seeded with <code>entries[..i] ++ [(op, x)]</code>. See <code>design/multishot-resume.md</code>. Consequence: handled bodies must be deterministic in their perform sequence (a mismatch names the op/index); non-op side effects in the prefix re-execute per <code>resume</code>.</li><li><strong>One-shot stays the default.</strong> A 1-ary clause <code>op: x ~&gt; e</code> keeps the direct-style reading (<code>resume</code> = the call returning); no journal is installed, so the cost is unchanged.</li><li><strong>Operational, not denotational.</strong> The paper&#39;s promonad-in-Prof is a proof artifact; handl&#39;s semantics is the operational interpreter. Same laws, different witness.</li><li><strong>Residual traces kept.</strong> Unhandled ops produce <code>IO&lt;{op:arg}&gt;</code> values — an inspectable trace the paper&#39;s model doesn&#39;t have (its ops must be handled). Keep: it&#39;s the &quot;effects as data&quot; property.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>runState</code>/<code>runWriter</code>/<code>runReader</code> become real <code>.hnl</code> combinators over <code>handle … acc</code> — the dogfood chained-IO workaround dies.</li><li><code>perform</code> without a handler is unchanged (mint residual).</li><li>Deep-carry (<code>handler_stack</code>, <code>handle_dyn</code>) is unchanged — the frames already exist; only <em>when</em> dispatch fires moves (at <code>perform</code>, not post-production).</li><li><code>IO</code>&#39;s sig type is unchanged; coverage checking (WS-F) is unchanged — it checks the <em>type</em>, which records ops regardless of interpretation.</li></ul><h2 id="non-goals" tabindex="-1">Non-goals <a class="header-anchor" href="#non-goals" aria-label="Permalink to “Non-goals”">​</a></h2><ul><li>One-shot continuations only (see above; multi-shot is delivered by replay, not continuation capture).</li><li>Shallow handlers (deep-carry covers; shallow adds no expressiveness).</li><li><code>extern</code> FFI-op decl form — separate phase-4 item.</li></ul>`,29)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};